  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 250 AM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\n===== Weekend Outlook =====\n\nThe upper trough axis will shift east over the weekend, allowing \nupstream ridging to begin to slightly amplify and pivot across the \nmiddle of the US. At the sfc, high pressure will spread out across \nthe eastern US, potentially backbuilding to the west on Sunday. This \nwould result in better WAA on Sunday with deeper SW flow. We'll \nremain dry and sunny over the weekend, with highs in the upper 80s \non Saturday and potentially lower 90s by Sunday. \n\n===== Heating Up Next Week =====\n\nWe'll remain under broad upper level ridging through much of next \nweek, with any shortwave activity riding over the ridge and across \nsouthern Canada. This pattern will keep us dry through at least \nTuesday night, but possibly through Wednesday too. Sfc high pressure \nwill remain in our east, resulting in a persistent WAA regime \nunderneath the upper ridge. We'll likely see temps climb back to the \n90s for next week, but just how hot we get remains uncertain. The \nGEFS mean ensemble does indicate potential for reaching the mid 90s \nby Wednesday, with a similar indication from the EPS. Combining the \nglobal ensembles, the LREF mean ensemble suggests mid 90s for the \nmiddle part of next week, especially Wednesday and Thursday. LREF \nprobability of exceeding 95F is only 25% in Tuesday, but climbs to \nroughly 40% for Wednesday, and around 50% for Thursday. \nFortunately, our dewpoints will be in the upper 60s to low 70s next \nweek, which will help keep our heat indices below 105F for most. \n\nThere is high confidence we'll have above normal temps next week, \nbut confidence is low on how hot temps could get and the resulting \nheat indices.",
